INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "HyperFund/HyperVerse benchmark evidence batch", "source_url": "SRC-004; SRC-003; SRC-001; SRC-002", "text": "Benchmark-returned evidence: SEC complaint alleges HyperFund was a global crypto-asset multi-level-marketing pyramid and Ponzi scheme that raised over $1.7 billion; it operated through projects collectively called HyperFund. HyperTech Group launched HyperFund in June 2020; stated goals evolved and it rebranded as HyperVerse around December 2021 and HyperNation around May 2022. HyperFund membership packages allegedly promised 0.5%-1% daily passive returns and potentially tripling initial investment in 600 days, plus referral rewards for recruiting investors. SEC complaint alleges no legitimate revenue source, no large-scale crypto mining despite promotional claims, withdrawals paid with new investor deposits, and an actor hired to pretend to be the new CEO at HyperVerse launch. It identifies Sam Lee as co-founder/central figure and Brenda Chunga aka Bitcoin Beautee as a top promoter; alleges Chunga recruited via seminars/videos, reached top promoter level requiring at least $5m in new investments, received over $3.7m, and displayed wealth to recruit others. SEC complaint also says HyperTech Group was presented as a blockchain conglomerate including Blockchain Global, HyperCash (formerly Hcash), CollinStar Capital, and DigitalX, and that founder/publicity credentials including CNN and Amazon Prime documentary appearances were used in credibility presentations. Guardian reports Steven Reece Lewis was introduced as HyperVerse CEO; universities, company registers, SEC filings, Adobe acquisition records, and Goldman Sachs employment records reportedly had no matching records, and no independent internet presence was found. Guardian reports celebrity support videos from Steve Wozniak, Chuck Norris, Jim Norton and Lance Bass were released during recruitment; it says no suggestion celebrities knew the business model. Guardian describes memberships starting at $300, 0.5% daily minimum returns/300% over 600 days, reinvestment incentives, and multi-level referral commissions. Guardian reports members faced withdrawal problems and were encouraged to migrate to HyperNation; a promoter said HyperVerse and HyperNation were 'one big entity,' while Lee denied involvement in HyperNation. CNBC/DOJ report criminal charges and allegations: Sam Lee charged with conspiracy to commit securities and wire fraud; Brenda Chunga pleaded guilty to conspiracy to commit securities and wire fraud; Rodney Burton charged over alleged unlicensed money transmission. DOJ says HyperFund was also known as HyperTech, HyperCapital, HyperVerse and HyperNation; alleges false mining claims, 0.5%-1% daily rewards, and withdrawals blocked beginning at least July 2021. DOJ explicitly notes indictment is an allegation and defendants are presumed innocent."}
